0 stars\nL155: \nL156: ### Watchers\nL157: \nL158: 0 watching\nL159: \nL160: ### Forks\nL161: \nL162: 【90†0 forks】\nL163: \nL164: 【91†Report repository】\nL165: \nL166: main\nL167: \nL168: 【92†Branches】【93†Tags】\nL169: \nL170: [Input: Go to file]\nL171: \nL172: Go to file\nL173: \nL174: Code\nL175: \nL176: Open more actions menu\nL177: ## Latest commit\nL178: \nL179: \nL180: \nL181: ## History\nL182: \nL183: 4 Commits\nL184: ## Folders and files\nL185: \nL186: Name  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\nL187: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL188: 【94†data/suitesparse】  | 【94†data/suitesparse】  |    |\nL189: 【95†plot】  | 【95†plot】  |    |\nL190: 【96†results】  | 【96†results】  |    |\nL191: 【97†scripts】  | 【97†scripts】  |    |\nL192: 【98†src】  | 【98†src】  |    |\nL193: 【99†.gitattributes】  | 【99†.gitattributes】  |    |\nL194: 【100†.gitignore】  | 【100†.gitignore】  |    |\nL195: 【101†Makefile】  | 【101†Makefile】  |    |\nL196: 【102†README.md】  | 【102†README.md】  |    |\nL197: ## Repository files navigation\nL198: \nL199:   *   * 【103†README】\nL200: \nL201: More items\nL202: \nL203: # Cache-Aware and GPU-Accelerated Sparse Matrix-Vector Multiplication (SpMV)\nL204: \nL205: End-to-end SpMV optimization spanning CPU (cache-tiled, AVX2 + software prefetching) and GPU (CUDA) implementations, benchmarked on Northeastern's Explorer cluster against synthetic matrices and four real-world matrices from the SuiteSparse Matrix Collection.\nL206: ## Headline result\nL207: \nL208: Hand-written CUDA `vector` kernel beats NVIDIA's cuSPARSE library by 24-56 percent on regular real-world matrices and reaches 98-110 percent of A100 HBM2 peak bandwidth. GPU is 8-273x faster than the best CPU implementation depending on matrix size.\nL209: ### GPU bandwidth on SuiteSparse matrices (A100, peak 1555 GB/s)\nL210: \nL211: Matrix  | rows  | nnz  | vector kernel  | cuSPARSE  | vector vs cuSPARSE\nL212: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL213: cant  | 62K  | 4.0M  | 1531 GB/s  | 1172 GB/s  | +30.6%\nL214: consph  | 83K  | 6.0M  | 1557 GB/s  | 1252 GB/s  | +24.4%\nL215: pdb1HYS  | 36K  | 4.3M  | 1718 GB/s  | 1102 GB/s  | +55.8%\nL216: webbase-1M  | 1.0M  | 3.1M  | 203 GB/s  | 847 GB/s  | cuSPARSE wins\nL217: On highly irregular web-graph matrices (max nnz/row >> avg), the kernel selector correctly skips ELL and cuSPARSE's adaptive scheduling wins.\nL218: ### CPU vs GPU (kernel-only) on synthetic matrices\nL219: \nL220: GPU achieves 8-273x speedup over the best CPU implementation (best of baseline / cache-tiled / AVX2 SIMD with software prefetching). Speedup scales with matrix size: tiny matrices (10K rows) get modest 8-12x because GPU launch overhead dominates; larger matrices (100K rows) get 200-273x as parallelism takes over.\nL221: \nL222: ## Implementations\nL223: ### CPU\nL224: \nL225:   * Baseline CSR SpMV (naive row-major)\nL226:   * Cache-tiled SpMV (blocked iteration, L2 reuse)\nL227:   * AVX2 SIMD with software prefetching (256-bit vectors, configurable pfd)\nL228: ### GPU (CUDA)\nL229: \nL230:   * CSR scalar: 1 thread per row (uncoalesced, divergent on irregular rows)\nL231:   * CSR vector: 1 warp per row, coalesced loads, warp-shuffle reduction\nL232:   * ELL: column-major padded layout, fully coalesced\nL233:   * cuSPARSE CSR_ALG2: NVIDIA library reference\nL234: The harness automatically skips ELL when (a) it would consume more than half of free GPU memory, or (b) the row distribution is too irregular (max_nnz_per_row / avg > 50 and max > 100). This avoids OOM on large graph matrices and avoids running a strictly-worse algorithm.\nL235: ## Build\nL236: \nL237:     make cpu                       # gcc -O3 -march=native -ftree-vectorize\nL238:     make gpu                       # nvcc -arch=sm_80 (A100, default)\nL239:     make gpu CUDA_ARCH=sm_70       # V100\nL240:     make gpu CUDA_ARCH=sm_90       # H100\nL241: \nL242: GPU build needs CUDA 12.x. Tested with `nvcc 12.3` on Explorer.\nL243: \nL244: ## Run\nL245: \nL246: ### Single kernel, single matrix\nL247: \nL248:     ./src/spmv_gpu --kernel vector \\\nL249:                    --matrix data/suitesparse/cant.mtx \\\nL250:                    --validate --iters 20\nL251: ### Full sweep (CPU perf + cachegrind + GPU, all kernels, all matrices)\nL252: \nL253:     make full                                          # default A100\nL254:     sbatch --gres=gpu:v100-sxm2:1 \\\nL255:            --export=ALL,CUDA_ARCH=sm_70 \\\nL256:            scripts/run_all.sh                          # V100 fallback\nL257: \nL258: Skip phases via env vars: `SKIP_CPU=1`, `SKIP_GPU=1`, `SKIP_CACHEGRIND=1`.\nL259: \nL260: ### Kernel-only CPU benchmark (for fair CPU vs GPU comparison)\nL261: \nL262:     bash scripts/cpu_bench.sh        # writes results/cpu/timings.csv\nL263: ### CLI flags (`spmv_gpu`)\nL264: \nL265: flag  | purpose\nL266: --- | ---\nL267: `--kernel scalar|vector|ell|cusparse|all`  | which kernel(s) to run\nL268: `--matrix <path>`  | accepts custom `.csr` or SuiteSparse `.mtx`\nL269: `--iters N`  | timed iterations after 1 warmup (default 10)\nL270: `--csv <file>`  | append timings as CSV\nL271: `--validate`  | compare GPU output against CPU CSR reference\nL272: ## Test matrices\nL273: \nL274: `data/*.csr` synthetic, generated by `csr_generator.c` at sizes 10K, 50K, 100K rows with target sparsity 1, 5, 10 percent. Note: the percent label is a generator parameter, not actual density (sparsity 5 produces ~2 nnz per row). These are useful for correctness checks but too small and too uniform to expose real GPU performance.\nL275: `data/suitesparse/*.mtx` real-world matrices from the SuiteSparse Matrix Collection. See `data/suitesparse/README.md` for download instructions. This is where the kernel comparison is meaningful.\nL276: ## Analysis and plots\nL277: \nL278:     python3 scripts/roofline.py --csv results/gpu/timings.csv --peak 1555\nL279:     python3 plot/generate_gpu_plots.py\nL280:     python3 plot/generate_cpu_gpu_compare.py\nL281:     python3 plot/generate_plots.py                # CPU perf plots\nL282: \nL283: `roofline.py` prints per-(matrix, kernel) achieved bandwidth, percent of peak, and the best kernel per matrix. Override `--peak` for V100 (900) or H100 (3350).\nL284: \nL285: ## Findings\nL286: ### CPU (Linux perf, synthetic matrices)\nL287: \nL288:   * Baseline CSR is memory-latency dominated, highest CPI\nL289:   * Cache tiling cuts runtime ~18 percent via L2 reuse\nL290:   * AVX2 + software prefetching at pfd=16 yields ~30 percent improvement over baseline by overlapping memory latency with vectorized compute\nL291:   * Tile sizes 128 to 256 are most stable across matrix sizes\nL292: ### GPU (A100, SuiteSparse matrices)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove · GitHub (https://github.com/Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove/releases)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove/releases\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 140\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL15:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL16:       * 【6†CodespacesInstant dev environments】\nL17:       * 【7†IssuesPlan and track work】\nL18:       * 【8†Code ReviewManage code changes】\nL19:       * 【9†Code QualityEnforce quality at merge】\nL20:     * APPLICATION SECURITY\nL21:       * 【10†GitHub Advanced SecurityFind and fix vulnerabilities】\nL22:       * 【11†Code securitySecure your code as you build】\nL23:       * 【12†Secret protectionStop leaks before they start】\nL24: \nL25:     * EXPLORE\nL26:       * 【13†Why GitHub】\nL27:       * 【